    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Prince Pedro declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> independent of Portugal in 1822 and ruled it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a>.</li><li>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> the monarchy held a vast country together, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> grown by enslaved people drove its wealth.</li><li>Abolition in 1888 cost it the planters' support, and an army coup ended the empire in 1889 almost unopposed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> studied languages including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupi">Tupi</a>, and corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-pasteur">Louis Pasteur</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

